.Language=English,English
.PluginContents=Panel Tabs Redux
.Options CtrlStartPosChar=^<wrap>

@Contents
$ #Panel Tabs Redux#
 Panel Tabs Redux adds tabs to the left and right panels. Each panel has its
own list of tabs, and the lists are saved between sessions.

 To open the tab list (the picker), choose #Panel Tabs Redux# from #Alt+F1#
for the left panel or #Alt+F2# for the right panel. You can also open it for
the active panel from the ~plugins menu~@:PluginCommands@ (#F11#) or by typing
#ptabs:# on the command line. If the entry is missing from #Alt+F1# or #Alt+F2#,
press #F9# there and enable #Show plugins#.

   ~The tab picker~@Picker@
   ~Editing a tab~@EditTab@
   ~Pinned and unpinned tabs~@Pinning@
   ~Archives and plugin panels~@Locations@
   ~Hotkeys and commands~@Commands@

@Picker
$ #Tab picker#
 The picker lists the tabs of one panel. The checkmark shows the current tab.
Each line shows the tab's shortcut, its tags, its name, and its location.

 #Enter#              ^<wrap>Switch to the highlighted tab.
 #1#-#9#, #A#-#Z#     ^<wrap>Switch to the tab with that shortcut.
 #Insert#             ^<wrap>Add a tab. For a folder, you are asked for its
path, starting with the current directory. In an archive or a plugin panel, the
tab is added for the current location. A new tab does not become current until
you select it.
 #F4#                 ^<wrap>~Edit~@EditTab@ the highlighted tab.
 #F3#                 ^<wrap>~Pin or unpin~@Pinning@ the highlighted tab.
 #Delete#             ^<wrap>Close the highlighted tab. Each panel keeps at
least one tab.
 #Ctrl+Up#, #Ctrl+Down#  ^<wrap>Move the highlighted tab. Its shortcut does not
change.
 #Tab#                ^<wrap>Show the other panel's tabs.
 #Esc#                ^<wrap>Close the picker.

 Tags:

 #[Pinned]#           ^<wrap>The tab keeps its saved directory. See
~Pinned and unpinned tabs~@Pinning@.
 #[Open only]#        ^<wrap>The tab points to something that cannot be
reopened later. See ~Archives and plugin panels~@Locations@.

 See also: ~Contents~@Contents@

@EditTab
$ #Editing a tab#
 Press #F4# in the ~picker~@Picker@ to change a tab.

 #Name#               ^<wrap>A label shown in the picker. Leave it empty to
show only the location.
 #Path#               ^<wrap>The tab's directory. If you change the path of the
current tab, the panel goes there. Inside an archive, this is the folder in the
archive, and an empty path means the archive root. Tabs that point to an open
panel, such as the Temporary Panel, have no editable path.
 #Shortcut#           ^<wrap>One key from #1#-#9# or #A#-#Z#. Leave it empty to
remove the shortcut. New tabs get the first free key automatically.
 #Pinned#             ^<wrap>Keep the saved directory while you browse. See
~Pinned and unpinned tabs~@Pinning@.

 If the shortcut is already used by another tab on the same panel, choose
#Use anyway# to move it to this tab, or #Cancel# to go back to the editor.

 See also: ~Contents~@Contents@

@Pinning
$ #Pinned and unpinned tabs#
 An #unpinned# tab follows you. When you browse to another directory, the
current tab is updated to that directory. New tabs are unpinned.

 A #pinned# tab keeps its saved directory while you browse. Select it again
to go back to that directory. To change the saved directory, use ~F4~@EditTab@.

 Press #F3# in the picker to pin or unpin a tab. If you unpin the current tab,
it takes the directory you are in now.

 When you switch away from a tab, it remembers the file under the cursor for
the rest of the session.

 See also: ~Contents~@Contents@

@Locations
$ #Archives and plugin panels#
 #Archives#

 Tabs work inside archives that FAR can open. #Insert# adds a tab for the
current folder in the archive. An unpinned tab follows you into and out of
archives just as it follows ordinary folders.

 FAR extracts an archive inside another archive to a temporary file. Tabs in
such archives are marked #[Open only]# and work only while that archive is
open on the panel. After it closes, open it again from the outer archive.

 #Plugin panels#

 The picker and hotkeys also work on plugin panels. If the plugin provides a folder shortcut, the tab can open the
location again later.

 Panels without folder shortcuts, such as the Temporary Panel, get
#[Open only]# tabs that work only while the panel is open. On such a panel,
#Insert# asks for a folder path to add instead, and #Ctrl+Alt+T# adds an
#[Open only]# tab. To switch tabs on the other
side without closing the panel, press #Tab# in the picker.

 Folder shortcuts can contain user names and passwords. For that reason, the
saved tabs are encrypted for your Windows account.

 See also: ~Contents~@Contents@

@Commands
$ #Hotkeys and commands#
 The macro script #PanelTabs.lua# adds these hotkeys. They work when the
command line is empty.

 #Ctrl+Alt+Space#     ^<wrap>Open the picker.
 #Ctrl+Alt+T#         ^<wrap>Add a tab for the current location and switch
to it.
 #Ctrl+Alt+W#         ^<wrap>Close the current tab.
 #Ctrl+Alt+PgUp#      ^<wrap>Previous tab.
 #Ctrl+Alt+PgDn#      ^<wrap>Next tab.
 #Alt+Shift+1#-#9#, #A#-#Z#  ^<wrap>Switch to the tab with that shortcut.

 To install the script, copy it to #%FARPROFILE%\Macros\scripts# and run
#macro:load#. To change the keys, edit the script.

 The same actions are available from the command line:

 #ptabs:#, #ptabs:list#  ^<wrap>Open the picker.
 #ptabs:new#          ^<wrap>Add a tab for the current location and switch
to it.
 #ptabs:close#        ^<wrap>Close the current tab.
 #ptabs:prev#         ^<wrap>Previous tab.
 #ptabs:next#         ^<wrap>Next tab.
 #ptabs:1#, #ptabs:A#, ...  ^<wrap>Switch to the tab with that shortcut.

 See also: ~Contents~@Contents@
